@startuml "Bridge - Pattern Structure - Class Diagram"
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
title Bridge — pattern structure (Design Patterns, p. 153)


class Client

abstract class Abstraction {
  # imp : IImplementor
  + Operation() : void
}
note right of Abstraction
  Defines the abstraction's interface,
  and maintains a reference to an
  object of type IImplementor.
  Operation() is implemented as
  imp.OperationImp() — a <b>higher-level</b>
  operation built from primitives.
end note

class RefinedAbstraction {
  + Operation() : void
  + ExtendedOperation() : void
}
note bottom of RefinedAbstraction
  Extends the interface defined
  by Abstraction. Knows nothing
  about any implementation.
end note

interface IImplementor {
  + OperationImp() : void
}
note right of IImplementor
  Defines the interface for
  implementation classes. It does <b>not</b>
  have to match Abstraction's interface —
  typically it provides only <b>primitive</b>
  operations (p. 154).
end note

class ConcreteImplementorA {
  + OperationImp() : void
}
class ConcreteImplementorB {
  + OperationImp() : void
}

Client ..> Abstraction : uses
Abstraction o--> "imp" IImplementor : delegates to
Abstraction <|-- RefinedAbstraction
IImplementor <|.. ConcreteImplementorA
IImplementor <|.. ConcreteImplementorB

note as N1
  <b>Two hierarchies, one link.</b>
  Left: what the thing is, and what it can do.
  Right: how it is actually carried out.
  Neither inherits from the other, so each can
  grow without the other being touched — and the
  link is a <b>reference</b>, so it is chosen while
  the program runs.
end note
N1 .. Abstraction

@enduml
